        <briefdescription>
<para>Produces a self-describing Argon2id hash string for <computeroutput>password</computeroutput>. </para>
        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para>Uses a random salt generated by libsodium. The cost parameters are deliberately low enough for the dedicated worker pool but meet OWASP&apos;s minimum recommendations. In everyday words: we make a random salt, mix it with the password using the Argon2id recipe, and return one string holding the recipe settings, the salt and the result. &quot;nullopt&quot; means &quot;no value&quot; and is returned when the machine cannot spare the needed memory. <parameterlist kind="param"><parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername direction="in">password</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>Plaintext password to hash. </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
</parameterlist>
<simplesect kind="return"><para>The encoded hash string on success, or <computeroutput>std::nullopt</computeroutput> if libsodium could not allocate the required memory (a critical message is logged in that case). </para>
</simplesect>
<simplesect kind="note"><para>Safe to call concurrently; no per-instance state is mutated.</para>
</simplesect>
Hashes <computeroutput>password</computeroutput> into a self-describing Argon2id string. In everyday words: ask libsodium to pick a random salt and do the slow mixing, writing a ready-to-store string into a fixed-size buffer. password: Plaintext password. Returns Encoded hash on success; <computeroutput>std::nullopt</computeroutput> if libsodium could not allocate memory. See also: <ref refid="classPasswordCrypt_1aaa1e7f7eec2be8279ba7330313001546" kindref="member">PasswordCrypt::hashPassword</ref> </para>
        </detaileddescription>
