    <briefdescription>
<para>Thin wrapper around libsodium&apos;s password-hashing primitives used by the password workers. </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>Owns no per-instance state beyond ensuring libsodium is initialized in the constructor. Hashing uses Argon2id at OWASP&apos;s minimum recommended cost; verification also accepts hashes produced by the legacy scrypt scheme so existing accounts remain valid. The stored hash is self-describing (algorithm, cost, salt and digest), which is how verifyPassword selects the algorithm without any extra metadata.</para>
<para>In everyday words: a &quot;hash&quot; is a one-way, deliberately slow, scrambled version of a password. We store the hash and never the real password. &quot;Argon2id&quot; and &quot;scrypt&quot; are the names of the recipes used to scramble it. &quot;Self-describing&quot; means the hash string itself records the recipe and the amount of work used, so verification needs no separate note. A &quot;salt&quot; is random data mixed in so two people with the same password still get different hashes. <simplesect kind="warning"><para>The hashes are intentionally expensive; callers must run <ref refid="classPasswordCrypt_1aaa1e7f7eec2be8279ba7330313001546" kindref="member">hashPassword()</ref>/verifyPassword() on a <ref refid="classPasswordWorker" kindref="compound">PasswordWorker</ref>, never on a Drogon IO thread. See <ref refid="passwordworker_8hpp_1a11301e300507b2f5ba81930e709a5de6" kindref="member">runOnPasswordWorker()</ref>. </para>
</simplesect>
</para>
    </detaileddescription>
